{"@odata.context":"$metadata#Directions/$entity","booleans":{},"counters":{},"entity_id":"en-019d95ab-c435-70a2-8067-7e23b31eb5d4","entity_type":"Direction","events":[{"action":"Created","from_status":"","params":{},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T09:42:35.318151Z","to_status":"Proposed"},{"action":"Propose","from_status":"Proposed","params":{"counterfactual_summary":"If this is wrong, better models alone will overcome review bottlenecks and organizations will absorb large increases in agent-generated output without major workflow or role changes.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d95ab-488b-7a82-a0c6-1cd7dde2de96","en-019d95ab-63b3-7ad1-8cdd-9d165b498150"]","projection_id":"en-019d95aa-b3d9-77c3-8da3-2d8b6860d48b","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d95aa-b431-7151-ba0c-d4babec5ff45","reasoning":"Organizational/adoption direction: in the next 0-180 days, the main adoption ceiling for directed software evolution will be managerial redesign, not model capability. Enterprises can already generate more code suggestions and pull requests than their review systems can absorb, so the bottleneck shifts to who owns review authority, how teams measure productivity, and whether platform groups can create safe operating norms. This is why many visible deployments will remain framed as copilots or constrained agents even when the underlying models are capable of more aggressive autonomy.

